<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Léogâne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Léogâne is a coastal town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-au-prince">Port-au-Prince</a>. It stands near the site of Yaguana, seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdom</a> of Jaragua, and under French rule it was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>-producing district and for a time the capital of the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>. Close to the epicentre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/2010-haiti-earthquake">2010 Haiti earthquake</a>, it was largely destroyed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leogane.jpg" alt="Léogâne" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Majistra Romulus · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_Leogane.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Léogâne lies about 30 km west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-au-prince">Port-au-Prince</a> in the Ouest Department, bounded to the north by the Gulf of Gonâve and to the south by the summit of the Massif de la Selle. Ten of its thirteen communal sections lie in the mountains. The commune has six rivers, of which the Momance is the most important for irrigation, and its economy rests mainly on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a>, bananas, grains and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When Europeans arrived in 1492, Yaguana was the capital of Jaragua, one of the five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>. Jaragua held out against the Spanish conquest until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anacaona">Anacaona</a>, born there in 1474, was captured and killed in 1503. In 1592 an English privateer fleet led by Christopher Newport captured and burned the town. Under French rule the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-du-casse">Jean-Baptiste du Casse</a>, appointed in 1691, gave the area the name Léogâne, which keeps traces of the Taíno name.</p><p>The town was destroyed by an earthquake in 1770. In 1791 and 1792 Romaine-la-Prophétesse, who owned a plantation outside the town, led rebels who took control of it, destroyed nearby plantations and freed their slaves. In 1803, late in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-dessalines">Jean-Jacques Dessalines</a> had the town burned to force out the last French colonists. His wife Marie-Claire Heureuse Félicité, later Empress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, was born there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne-peralte">Charlemagne Péralte</a>, who led resistance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> occupation that began in 1915, had been an officer stationed in Léogâne.</p><p>The earthquake of 12 January 2010, of magnitude 7.0, damaged 80 to 90 per cent of the town's buildings, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> team called it the worst affected area. The military estimated that 20,000 to 30,000 people died there. The Sainte Rose de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> church and school, the Saint Croix Hospital and the court building were destroyed or badly damaged. With no airport, aid arrived by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helicopter">helicopter</a> and small planes, and Canadian, Japanese, Argentine, Cuban and South Korean teams set up field hospitals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Léogâne is known as the centre of Rara, a festival of parading bands with drums and bamboo horns that runs from Carnival through Lent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> week. Its historic sites include Fort Campan and an old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windmill">windmill</a> at Baussan, and many pilgrims visit its patron saints each year.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Léogâne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its paramount cacique Bohechío ruled from a town near modern <b>Léogâne</b>, receiving tribute from many lesser chiefs.</blockquote><a href="/state/cacicazgo-of-jaragua">Cacicazgo of Jaragua</a></li></ul></section>
